\section{Scores}{


sheepdog works with scores between 0 and 1, as BirdNET writes them.
Perch writes logits, and OpenSoundscape returns logits unless \code{predict()}
was called with \code{activation_layer = "sigmoid"} or \code{"softmax"}. Neither
file records the scale, so it is given in \code{score_scale}: with \code{"logit"}
(the default of both classifiers), \code{confidence} is the logistic function
of the score, \code{plogis(score)}; with \code{"probability"}, the score is used as
it is and must lie between 0 and 1. The calibration models work on the
logit of \code{confidence}, which for logits is the score itself, so no
information is lost. The original score is kept in \code{score}.

Rows with \code{confidence} below \code{min_confidence} are left out (0.1, the
default minimum confidence of BirdNET). A Perch file already lacks the
windows below the logit threshold used to write it; a logit of 0 is a
confidence of 0.5.
}

\section{Sites and labels}{


The site is the folder that holds each audio file (\code{site_A/rec.wav}
gives \code{site_A}), or is taken from the file name with \code{site_pattern}, as
in \code{\link[=read_selection_tables]{read_selection_tables()}}. The date and hour of a recording are read
from its file name (\code{YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS}) by the functions downstream, as for
BirdNET.

sheepdog names the folder of a species after the part of \code{species_code}
after the last underscore. Labels of other classifiers are names or codes
without that part, so \code{species_code} is built as the label and its
lower-case form with every run of other characters replaced by a hyphen:
\code{"Boana raniceps"} gives \code{"Boana raniceps_boana-raniceps"}, whose folder
is \code{boana_raniceps}. The label is kept in \code{label}.
}

